.TH "PW-LOUDCOMP" "5" "2026-10-18"
.SH NAME
.PD
.PP
\fBpw-loudcomp\fP \- settings file for pw\-loudcomp
.SH SYNOPSIS
.PD
.PP
\fB$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/pw-loudcomp/settings.env\fP
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PD
.PP
The settings file for \fBpw-loudcomp\fP\&.
.SH VARIABLES
.TP
\fBinput_gain\fP \fIinteger\fP in dB
Controls the amount of input gain to achieve the listening volume\&.

.TP
\fBlistening_volume\fP \fIinteger\fP in dB SPL
The volume level to correct to; it can be set to \fIalmost\fP any volume\&.

.TP
\fBvolume_curve_exponent\fP \fIfloat\fP
This setting controls how much the volume changes to feel natural; Windows uses 2, while most Linux slider implementations use 3\&.

.TP
\fBprocessing_mode\fP \fIinteger\fP
0 = FFT; 1 = IIR

.TP
\fBfft_quality\fP \fIinteger\fP
Controls the buffer size of the FFT algorithm; higher is better quality but with increased processing time\&. 0 = 256; 1 = 512; 2 = 1024; 3 = 2048; 4 = 4096; 5 = 8192; 6 = 16384

.TP
\fBiir_approx\fP \fIinteger\fP
Specifies the approximation of the IIR algorithm; higher is better quality but with higher CPU usage\&. 0 = Fastest; 1 = Low; 2 = Normal; 3 = High; 4 = Best

.TP
\fBcurve\fP \fIinteger\fP
Specifies the curve type\&. 0 = Flat; 1 = ISO226\-2003; 2 = Fletcher\-Munson; 3 = Robinson\-Dadson; 4 = ISO226\-2023

.TP
\fBis_clipping\fP \fIinteger\fP
Whether to enable/disable clipping\&.

.TP
\fBclipping_range\fP \fIinteger\fP in dB
Specifies a clipping range\&.

.TP
\fBoutput_device\fP \fIstring\fP
Specifies which device to output to\&. auto = makes the script use the default output device; \fIdevice_name\fP = uses a specific device

.TP
\fBoutput_device_fallback\fP \fIstring\fP
Specifies a fallback device to output to\&. auto = makes the script use the default output device; \fIdevice_name\fP = uses a specific device; none = disables fallback

.TP
\fBsystem_transfer\fP \fIboolean\fP
Whether to transfer volume from PipeWire to the plugin and vice versa\&.

.TP
\fBsystem_fallback\fP \fIboolean\fP
Whether to control volume in PipeWire when the daemon is not running; useful for shortcuts\&.

.TP
\fBauto_adjust_volume\fP \fIboolean\fP
Whether to auto\-adjust volume when the input gain changes\&.

.TP
\fBauto_reload\fP \fIboolean\fP
Whether to auto\-reload on file save\&.

.TP
\fBauto_switch\fP \fIboolean\fP
Whether to auto\-switch devices when the PipeWire default device changes while the daemon is running; only works when \fBoutput_device\fP is set to auto\&.

.TP
\fBhighest_input_gain_limit\fP \fIinteger\fP in dB
Sets an upper limit to the input gain; leave empty for no limit\&.

.TP
\fBlowest_input_gain_limit\fP \fIinteger\fP in dB
Sets a lower limit to the input gain; leave empty for no limit\&.

.TP
\fBhighest_volume_limit\fP \fIinteger\fP in percent
Sets an upper limit to the volume; leave empty for no limit\&.

.TP
\fBlowest_volume_limit\fP \fIinteger\fP in percent
Sets a lower limit to the volume; leave empty for no limit\&.

.SH CUSTOM INPUT FOR EACH DEVICE
.PD
.PP
By adding a variable with the same name as a device name followed by the \fC_input_gain\fP suffix, the script will use that input gain instead of the value in \fBinput_gain\fP\&.
.PD
.PP
Note: if the device name contains \&"\&.\&" or \&"\-\&", these characters need to be replaced with \&"_\&" to work\&.
.SH SEE ALSO
.PD
.PP
pw\-loudcomp(1)
